---
import Layout from "../layouts/BlogPost.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="How I Develop"
  description="TakashiAihara's development workflow with parallel AI coding agents"
  pubDate={new Date("October 15 2026")}
>
  <p>
    I run many Claude Code sessions in parallel and keep myself in the loop only
    where a human decision matters. Most of the workflow below is backed by
    tools I built and publish on GitHub.
  </p>

  <h2>One session, one scope</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Each agent session owns exactly one task and works in its own clone of the repository.</li>
    <li>
      Clones are created by <a href="https://github.com/TakashiAihara/ccx">ccx</a>
      as fully independent copies in under a second, so several sessions can
      check out the same branch without git worktrees.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every session is attached to a task on a self-hosted
      <a href="https://kaneo.app">Kaneo</a> board through
      <a href="https://github.com/TakashiAihara/kaneo-cli">kaneo-cli</a>, so I
      can see which session is working on what, and the next step is recorded
      before a session leaves.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Humans decide, agents keep moving</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Reversible choices are made by the agent and reported afterwards.</li>
    <li>Irreversible or outward-facing actions wait for my explicit approval.</li>
    <li>
      When a decision is needed, the agent queues it in a markdown file and keeps
      working on whatever does not depend on it. I answer with line comments in
      <a href="https://github.com/TakashiAihara/akapen">akapen</a>, which feeds
      the comments back to the session.
    </li>
    <li>
      Design discussions also happen in markdown reviewed line by line, not in a
      scrolling chat.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Rules as code</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Agent behavior lives in a versioned repository: global instructions,
      task-specific skills, hooks that block known mistakes, and small CLIs.
    </li>
    <li>Changes to those rules go through pull requests like any other code.</li>
    <li>
      After each scope, the session runs a retrospective and turns what went
      wrong into a rule, a skill or a tool, so the same mistake is not repeated.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Review and verification</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Every pull request gets an adversarial review by a dedicated agent playing
      the strictest human reviewer, plus independent reviews from other model
      families and CodeRabbit.
    </li>
    <li>
      Tests are checked with negative controls: the implementation is
      deliberately broken to confirm the test actually fails.
    </li>
    <li>
      When acceptance tests can be written first, implementation is delegated to
      a smaller model, while the main agent keeps the spec, the tests and the
      acceptance check.
    </li>
    <li>"Done" means the effect was observed, not that the code ran.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Everything leaves a record</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Investigations, setups and abandoned attempts all end as a markdown note
      merged into a knowledge repository.
    </li>
    <li>
      Session history is searchable with
      <a href="https://github.com/TakashiAihara/cchist">cchist</a>, and commits
      and pull requests carry a session id so any change can be traced back to
      the conversation that produced it.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Layout>
